      "dom_id": "question-19261",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14270407/assets/a1df1911a7636bde608156b957110bb296fc5cff3b88774e441d65fbd189970d.png)\n\nThe point $A$ with coordinates $(7,\\operatorname{ln}⁡7)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ and the point ${A}^{′}$ with coordinates $(3.5,\\operatorname{ln}⁡7)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax),$ shown above. What is the value of $a?$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax)$ can be obtained from $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ by a horizontal stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{a}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(7,\\operatorname{ln}⁡7)$ that lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ becomes \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{a}\\cdot 7,\\operatorname{ln}⁡7)=(3.5,\\operatorname{ln}⁡7)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡ax.$ \n\nTherefore, $a=2.$",
